Table 1 also shows that during the last operational stage of the A-reactor in which a constant HRT compared to stage III was applied but a higher COD vinasses concentration was fed (30 g L−1), accumulation of acetic acid (∼5.8 g L−1) was observed while propionate remained constant (∼1.8 g L−1). In this sense, a strong microbial dynamics from stage II to IV was observed (Fig. 3
), in which for instance bacteria from genus Prevotella was suppressed. Regarding biogas production, as expected only low production of methane was obtained (<2.0 L d−1), indicating an effective development of the acidogenic bacteria biofilm and a low abundance of methanogenic Archaea. The latter was corroborated by the Archaea/Bacteria ratio obtained by Q-PCR, which reached a value of 3% at the end of stage II. In addition, the biogas production rate increased but the gas phase consisted mainly of carbon dioxide. In this sense, according to the relative abundance of the archaeal population (Fig. 3), also a strong dynamics from stage II to IV was observed, becoming dominant the acetotrophic genus Methanosaeta (formerly Methanothrix) with a relative abundance of 39.7% and decreasing the relative abundance of the hydrogenotrophic genus Methanobacterium, from 99.4% to 45.5% (Fig. 4
).

Even though, the increases on the OLRs of the A-reactor promoted a slight accumulation of VFAs in the M-reactor, the results obtained in both reactors indicated a robust performance of the PBRs system. The biofilm configuration, which allowed retaining a high concentration of biomass, enabled the operation at an overall OLR up to 9.5 ± 1.4 g-COD L−1 d−1 (Table 2
                        ). Nevertheless, as it was previously mentioned, during the methanogenic phase the acetotrophic methanogenic populations were affected by the high acetic and propionic acid concentrations achieved during the last operational stage. Regardless of this detrimental effect, the biogas production trend was linear, achieving up to 1.35 L h−1 and confirming the robustness of this configuration for treating high OLRs. It is worth noticing that, the start-up strategy used in this study favored the prevalence of acidogenic populations in the A-reactor biofilm, decreasing syntrophic bacteria and methanogenic populations. In addition, this strategy impacted the methanogenic reactor with an increase of methanogenic population diversity, which resulted in the robustness of the M-reactor. Accordingly, the methane yields obtained (∼0.29 L CH4 g-COD−1, STP) were higher than those reported in previous studies for the treatment of vinasses in UASB reactors (0.22 L CH4 g-COD−1, STP) [28] and similar to those obtained in AnSBR reactors (< 0.3 L-CH4 g-CODadded
                        −1) [9]. In addition, despite that an inverse correlation of the total COD removal and the OLRs was observed (Table 2), the removal efficiencies of the present study were comparable with those reported for the anaerobic digestion of tequila vinasses (85%) in AnSBR operated at lower OLRs [9]. Higher COD removals (90%) have been also reported in CSTR [2] but operated at lower OLRs (1–6 g-COD L−1 d−1) and higher HRT (> 6 d).
